{"author":{"id":"b409816c5cd5ab140d1d52eb33aace35de44952bb794b19806fd57f6ecb1ace9"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1789489988,"nonce":"q0YcrkT0W6PlY4YZ0/uqvR1z4k0=","title":"First calls into the library compile on the program's own time","message":"A library function reached by the program is compiled on its first call (the caller waits on a thread with a full stack): bench_pipeline's run held 31 such compiles, 11.3 ms inside the compiles and 13.4 ms with the thread hop per call, out of 55 ms of run; bench_tokenize about 10 ms.\n\nNow, once the module is registered, a warm-up thread compiles the reachable library functions in the order the program is likely to call them (what its own functions call first, then what those call), through the same once-only path the first-call stub uses: a call arriving first compiles its own function, a call arriving during one waits on the lock and finds the entry. The thread stops between compiles when the backend shuts down, which joins it before anything it compiles into is released. `ZYNTAX_DISABLE_WARM_UP=1` leaves every first call to compile its function.\n\nMeasured on the M1 Pro under load: bench_pipeline run 55 to 52 ms; quiet numbers to follow. The warm-up compiles everything reachable (107 functions on bench_pipeline, about 40 ms of another core), most of it never called; a cap on how far from the program it goes would trade that for a first-call compile now and then.","files":null}]}